{% import "macros/breadcrumb.njk" as bc %}
{{ bc.trail(lang, [{label: "Map"}]) }}
<h1>Map</h1>
<p class="muted">
  Plot your waypoints (ground coordinates X/Z plus height) and link pairs joined by a translocator, then pick a source and
  destination to find the cheapest route: walking costs the straight-line distance, a translocator link costs nothing. Optionally
  draw the terrain you have explored behind them from the game's own map file, and click anywhere to see the climate a world
  seed generates there. Everything stays in this browser (download a JSON file to back your waypoints up or move them elsewhere).
  Ported from the standalone vintage-story-price-guide project, the launcher's map preview and the game's climate generator.
</p>

<div class="button-group">
  <button type="button" id="wpAdd">Add WayPoint</button>
  <button type="button" id="wpFit">Fit map</button>
  <button type="button" id="wpExport">Download JSON</button>
  <button type="button" id="wpClear">Clear all</button>
</div>

<div id="wpMap" class="waypoint-map-container" data-icons="{{ '/' | url }}assets/waypointIcons.json"></div>
<p class="muted">Drag to pan, scroll to zoom, click a dot to find it in the table below (use its Edit button to change it), or click a row in the table to centre the map on it. Dashed lines are translocator links; the highlighted line is the current route.</p>
<p id="wpPick" class="muted" role="status">Click an empty spot on the map to set the position used by the climate section below.</p>
<p id="wpEmpty" class="muted">No waypoints yet &mdash; add one to get started.</p>


<h2 id="load-data">Load your data</h2>
<label class="schematic-dropzone" for="wpImport">
  <strong>Upload waypoints</strong>
  <span class="muted">Drop a <code>.json</code> file here (your own export, or the translocator mod's <code>Nf3tData.json</code>), or click to browse</span>
  <input type="file" id="wpImport" accept=".json" hidden>
</label>
<label id="wpSaveRow" class="schematic-field" hidden>Savegame in mod data <select id="wpSave"></select></label>
<p id="wpStatus" class="muted" role="status"></p>

<details>
  <summary>Import from the translocator-locator mod</summary>
  <p class="muted">
    The <a href="https://github.com/jgwoolley/translocator-locator-cmd">translocator-locator</a> mod writes
    <code>Nf3tData.json</code> into the game's <code>ModData</code> folder. Upload it with the button above, then
    choose which savegame to load (savegames are identified by GUID; the one with the most entries is preselected).
    Translocators become linked waypoints (a game waypoint on the same spot is dropped), alongside the game's own waypoints, the world spawn and your last route. Coordinates are shown relative to the world spawn, as in the game.
    Default locations:
  </p>
  <ul class="muted">
    <li>Linux: <code>~/.config/VintagestoryData/ModData/</code></li>
    <li>Windows: <code>%appdata%\VintagestoryData\ModData\</code></li>
    <li>macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/VintagestoryData/ModData/</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">If you moved your data folder (the game's <code>--dataPath</code> option), look there instead.</p>
</details>

<h3 id="world-map">Explored world map</h3>
<label class="schematic-dropzone" for="wmFile">
  <strong>Load a world map file</strong>
  <span class="muted">Drop a <code>Maps/&lt;savegame id&gt;.db</code> file from the game's data folder here, or click to browse. It is read in your browser and never uploaded.</span>
  <input type="file" id="wmFile" hidden>
</label>
<p id="wmStatus" class="muted" role="status"></p>
<details>
  <summary>Where is that file?</summary>
  <p class="muted">The game's client keeps the map you have explored as one SQLite file per world, named after the savegame (for a server, after the
  server's world). Look in the <code>Maps</code> folder of the game's data folder:</p>
  <ul class="muted">
    <li>Linux: <code>~/.config/VintagestoryData/Maps/</code></li>
    <li>Windows: <code>%appdata%\VintagestoryData\Maps\</code></li>
    <li>macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/VintagestoryData/Maps/</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">The drawing is the map as the game saved it (one pixel per block); tiles you haven't visited stay empty. Waypoint coordinates are absolute
  world coordinates, so they line up with it (shown relative to the world spawn in the table, as in the game).</p>
</details>

<h2>Route</h2>
